phenylphosphonate or titanium phenylarsonate</article-title><source>Curr Anal Chem</source><year>2005</year><volume>1</volume><fpage>171</fpage><lpage>175</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.2174/1573411054021510</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhou</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Struve</surname><given-names>DM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The effects of post-persulfate-digestion procedures on total phosphorus analysis in water</article-title><source>Water Res</source><year>2004</year><volume>38</volume><fpage>3893</fpage><lpage>3898</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15380979</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.watres.2004.06.031</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p><bold>Visible spectra of solutions containing</bold>: 1, Sudan II (14 &#x003bc;M) in ethanol; 2, Sudan IV (6.3 &#x003bc;M) in ethanol; 3, lecithin liposomes (10 mg/ml).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-16-1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p><bold>Absorbance measurements of the solutions containing 0.20 mg/ml lecithin liposomes and Sudan II and IV and their linear regression</bold>. A: Sudan II in lecithin liposomes was dissolved in ethanol and the absorbance was measured at 495 nm; B: Sudan IV was dissolved in ethanol and the absorbance was measured at 515 nm; C: plots of <italic>&#x003b3;</italic><sup>-1 </sup>vs. C<sub>L</sub><sup>-1 </sup>for the interaction of Sudan II with liposomes; D: as C, but using Sudan IV in place of Sudan II.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-16-2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p><bold>Cartoon illustrating the proposed Sudan red-liposome binding</bold>. 1, Liposome sphere; 2, Sudan II &#x02013; liposome binding pattern involving hydrogen bond and hydrophobic bond; 3, Sudan II &#x02013; cell membrane binding pattern.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-16-3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p><bold>Effects of pH (A), electrolyte (B) and temperature (C) on the interactions of Sudan red compounds with lecithin liposomes (0.20 mg/l)</bold>. 1: 8 &#x003bc;M Sudan II and 2: 1 &#x003bc;M Sudan IV.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-16-4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p><bold>Growth curve of <italic>E. coli </italic>at 37&#x000b0;C</bold>. 1: turbidity of culture fluid at 600 nm at 48 h culture time; 2: variation of <italic>E. coli </italic>activity indicated by the absorbance measured at 555 nm with MTT.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-16-5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p><bold>Spectrophotometric measurements of the solutions containing <italic>E. coli </italic>and Sudan red compounds</bold>. 1: absorption of Sudan IV in extracellular solution; 2: absorption of Sudan IV accumulated in membrane; 3: absorption of Sudan II free in extracellular aqueous medium; 4: absorption of Sudan II accumulated in membrane; 5: absorption of Sudan IV in cytosol; 6: absorption of Sudan II in cytosol. B: distribution of Sudan II in the extracellular solution (red), membrane bilayer (blue) and cytosol (yellow). C: the equivalent information as given in B, but for Sudan IV.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-16-6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p><bold>Linear regression curves for the interactions of Sudan red compounds with <italic>E. coli</italic></bold>: A: Sudan II; B: Sudan IV.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-16-7"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p><bold>Cartoon illustrating the separation and detection procedures used to investigate the interaction between <italic>E. coli </italic>and Sudan red compounds (L)</bold>. (1) <italic>E. coli </italic>culture medium; (2) supernatant of (1) after centrifugation at 3500 &#x000d7; g; (3) <italic>E. coli </italic>collected; (4) <italic>E. coli </italic>liquid re-suspended with PBS still containing Sudan dyes (L) ; (5) supernatant of (4) after centrifugation at 3500 &#x000d7; g; (6) <italic>E. coli </italic>&#x02013; L binding substance collected; (7) <italic>E. coli </italic>&#x02013; L binding liquid re-suspended with PBS; (8) <italic>E. coli </italic>&#x02013; L binding liquid after ultrasonication; (9) supernatant of (8) after centrifugation at 5600 &#x000d7; g; (10) membrane &#x02013; L binding substance collected and (11) membrane &#x02013; L binding solution dissolved in organic solvent.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-16-8"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Standard curves for determination of Sudan red compounds in ethanol and pH 7.0 phosphate buffer solution (PBS)</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="left">Chemical</td><td align="center">Upper limit, nM</td><td align="center">Wave- length, nm</td><td align="center">Medium</td><td align="center">Linear equations</td><td align="center">Linear correlation coefficient</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Sudan II</td><td align="center">40</td><td align="center">495</td><td align="center">ethanol</td><td align="center"><italic>A </italic>= 0.0155<italic>C</italic><sub>L </sub>(2)</td><td align="center">0.9982</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Sudan II</td><td align="center">1.2</td><td align="center">495</td><td align="center">PBS *</td><td align="center"><italic>A </italic>= 0.0121<italic>C</italic><sub>L </sub>- 0.001 (3)</td><td align="center">0.9993</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Sudan IV</td><td align="center">0.6</td><td align="center">515</td><td align="center">ethanol</td><td align="center"><italic>A </italic>= 0.0318<italic>C</italic><sub>L </sub>- 0.0018 (4)</td><td align="center">0.9990</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Sudan IV</td><td align="center">5</td><td align="center">515</td><td align="center">PBS</td><td align="center"><italic>A </italic>= 0.0196<italic>C</italic><sub>L</sub>- 0.0007 (5)</td><td align="center">0.9990</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></sec></back></article>